 "text": "Hey there, how are you? An SSD stores data in flash memories, while a hard drive uses spinning disks. SSDs are faster and fewer failures, but they' also much more expensive per gigabyte. Yes, they' usually faster than hard drives for opening programs and scrolling through files. It depends on how much space you use now. I'd only switch if you' regularly waiting for things to load, or if your hard drive is getting full. SSDs don't really need one, since they don't have moving parts. If you want to back up your data, a simple USB drive is cheaper than cloud storage.",
